        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">01.</span> Black-Scholes Analytical Framework &amp; Inversion
        </h2>
        <p>
          Under the standard Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) assumptions, the theoretical price of a European call option C(S, K, T, r, &sigma;) is governed by the risk-neutral expected payoff:
        </p>
        
        <pre is:raw class="bg-[#0A101D] border border-slate-800 p-5 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300 overflow-x-auto my-4 text-center">
C = S * N(d1) - K * exp(-r * T) * N(d2)

where:
d1 = [ ln(S / K) + (r + 0.5 * sigma^2) * T ] / [ sigma * sqrt(T) ]
d2 = d1 - sigma * sqrt(T)
        </pre>

        <p>
          Because &sigma; cannot be expressed in closed algebraic form as an explicit function of C, quantitative desks solve the non-linear root-finding problem:
        </p>

        <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 border border-emerald-500/40 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto my-4 text-center">
f(sigma) = C_BS(S, K, T, r, sigma) - C_market = 0
        </pre>

        <p>
          The first derivative with respect to volatility is the option <strong>Vega</strong> (&nu;):
        </p>

        <pre is:raw class="bg-[#0A101D] border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-purple-300 overflow-x-auto my-4 text-center">
Vega = dC / d(sigma) = S * sqrt(T) * n(d1)
        </pre>

        <p>
          While Newton-Raphson iterations utilize &sigma;<sub>n+1</sub> = &sigma;<sub>n</sub> - [f(&sigma;<sub>n</sub>) / Vega(&sigma;<sub>n</sub>)], deep out-of-the-money options have near-zero Vega (&nu; &rarr; 0), causing gradient explosion and divergence. Brent's method (<code class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">scipy.optimize.brentq</code>) provides guaranteed bracketing convergence.
        </p>
      </section>

