          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">04.</span> Greeks Adaptation Across the Volatility Surface
        </h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          When options are priced using a flat volatility assumption rather than the dynamic surface, quantitative delta hedging introduces systematic error known as <strong>unhedged smile drift</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          In a local or stochastic volatility framework, the total delta includes the rate of change of implied volatility with respect to spot (the Vanna effect and skew slope):
        </p>

        <pre is:raw class="bg-[#0A101D] border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-cyan-300 overflow-x-auto my-4 text-center">
Delta_market = (dC_BS / dS) + (dC_BS / d(sigma)) * [ d(sigma_implied) / dS ]
             = Delta_BS + Vega * [ d(sigma_implied) / dS ]
        </pre>

        <p>
          Because equity volatility smiles typically display negative skew (d&sigma;<sub>imp</sub> / dS &lt; 0), standard Black-Scholes delta overestimates the risk of downward market moves, causing market makers to under-hedge short put gamma. For dynamic hedging models, review our guide on <a href="/options-gamma-scalping-theta-decay-hedging-strategies/" class="text-emerald-400 hover:underline font-semibold">Options Gamma Scalping &amp; Theta Decay</a>.
